Certificates: restrict the mode of private key files

UpdateTLSCertificate in internal/certificates/manage_libvirt.go writes
every file of the libvirt TLS Secret with os.WriteFile(target, data,
0644), the private key included. libvirt/private/serverkey.pem,
qemu/server-key.pem and ch/server-key.pem are therefore readable by
every local user of a hypervisor node, and the key authenticates the
node to every libvirtd of its migration domain (C5C3/cobaltcore#1175).
os.WriteFile keeps the mode of a file that already exists, so a
changed mode argument alone would not tighten a key an earlier
version wrote.

Every private key file now gets mode 0600. Each file is written to a
temporary file in the target's directory, and the temporary files are
renamed over their targets only once all of them are written: the
rename replaces a file whoever owns it and needs no CAP_FOWNER, no
reader sees a partly written key, and a write that fails leaves the
files of the previous Secret in place instead of a new certificate
beside an old key. A temporary file gets its group before its mode, so
the key is never readable by more users than the result. A key target
that a later commit adds to secretToFileMap gets mode 0600 too.

The secret controller calls UpdateTLSCertificate only when the
Secret's resourceVersion differs from the one it saved under
CA/.last_resource_version, so a node keeps the files an earlier
version wrote, with their mode, until its certificate is reissued. A
key every local user could read is better replaced than re-moded.

With native TLS (live_migration_with_native_tls), QEMU opens
qemu/server-key.pem itself, as the user it runs as, and Cloud
Hypervisor reads ch/server-key.pem the same way; that user need not be
root. When the new environment variable PKI_KEY_GROUP holds a numeric
group ID, these two keys get that group and mode 0640.
libvirt/private/serverkey.pem keeps mode 0600, but it holds the same
key, and libvirt's client key links to it, so the group can read the
key the node presents to libvirtd as well. The agent has to belong to
the group or hold CAP_CHOWN; otherwise the update fails with
"operation not permitted" and replaces no file. A group name is not
accepted, since the agent's image may have no group database, and an
invalid value fails the update before any file is written. Without
the variable no group changes. The chart value that sets PKI_KEY_GROUP
is left to the upstream submission.

The package had no test. TestUpdateTLSCertificateKeyMode walks the PKI
directory and fails for any file with the key's content that is not a
key target, for a key file with another mode or group, and for any
other file whose mode is not 0644. Its subtests cover new files, keys
an earlier version left at 0644, a second write, the key group,
invalid key groups, a missing key, an empty Secret and a failed
rename. TestUpdateTLSCertificateKeyGroupNotPermitted runs as a user
other than root and proves that a group the process does not belong
to fails the update and leaves every file of the previous Secret in
place and no temporary file behind; the CobaltCore image build runs it
as uid 65534.

Applies-to: cobaltcore-dev/kvm-node-agent main @ 1e4e4b8e7050bbfa2d5a990aa21b849ba7b51700

Upstream status: not submitted

